1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,299
Hi!

2
00:00:02,299 --> 00:00:06,915
This is the Walnut Internal Brain, our entry for Aspire's FinTech challenge.

3
00:00:06,915 --> 00:00:16,530
Ask one question across Confluence, Jira, Slack and Google Drive, and it answers only from what you are allowed to open, with every step logged.

4
00:00:16,530 --> 00:00:20,150
Tencent CodeBuddy helped build it, and it works inside WorkBuddy too.

5
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:23,192
We all lose hours hunting for things.

6
00:00:23,192 --> 00:00:27,807
The answer is in a ticket, a Slack thread, a runbook, somewhere.

7
00:00:27,807 --> 00:00:30,115
An AI assistant should fix that.

8
00:00:30,115 --> 00:00:40,115
But in a fintech, an assistant that can read everything can also leak everything, and showing the wrong person a fraud case can be a crime.

9
00:00:40,115 --> 00:00:47,038
So we built it around one simple rule: the model never sees anything the person asking can't see.

10
00:00:49,149 --> 00:00:51,073
Meet Jordan, a backend engineer.

11
00:00:51,073 --> 00:00:59,919
He asks one plain question, and gets one answer, pulled together from Slack, Jira and Confluence, with every sentence linked to its source.

12
00:00:59,919 --> 00:01:11,457
Now look at the strip under the answer: only sources Jordan can open, each one re-checked with the source system just before it was used, and the whole thing logged.

13
00:01:11,457 --> 00:01:16,073
There's also a blocker discussed in a leads-only channel Jordan isn't in.

14
00:01:16,073 --> 00:01:17,996
You won't find it here.

15
00:01:17,996 --> 00:01:20,303
It never even reached the model.

16
00:01:24,959 --> 00:01:28,036
This is Jordan's map: everything he can open.

17
00:01:28,036 --> 00:01:32,652
Eight hundred and sixty-seven items, each one checked live with its source.

18
00:01:32,652 --> 00:01:34,575
Ken, our contractor, sees twenty-four.

19
00:01:34,575 --> 00:01:39,575
And there's no "plus 843 hidden": for Ken, the rest simply doesn't exist.

20
00:01:40,810 --> 00:01:45,425
Here's the same question from Jordan and from Ken, side by side.

21
00:01:45,425 --> 00:01:47,348
Jordan gets the full picture.

22
00:01:47,348 --> 00:01:56,194
Ken gets a polite "I couldn't find that in the sources you can access", with no hint that there's anything more to find.

23
00:01:58,579 --> 00:02:06,656
And if you're new here, there's a short guided tour that walks you through all of this in about a minute.

24
00:02:12,180 --> 00:02:13,980
One more test.

25
00:02:13,980 --> 00:02:19,364
Ken asks about a real, restricted incident report, then about one we made up.

26
00:02:19,364 --> 00:02:21,672
The two answers are byte-for-byte identical.

27
00:02:21,672 --> 00:02:27,826
Our tests compare four hundred answers with and without the restricted files, and not one changes.

28
00:02:29,030 --> 00:02:33,260
The big fintech question is: what can I tell this customer?

29
00:02:33,260 --> 00:02:37,491
Amy, an account manager, asks why a customer's account is restricted.

30
00:02:37,491 --> 00:02:45,183
She gets the approved script, never the case notes, and it reads just like the answer for a made-up company.

31
00:02:45,183 --> 00:02:54,414
When Leo from ops asks what we can offer an Indonesian SME, he gets that market's answer, from the approved policy, never a draft.

32
00:02:55,680 --> 00:02:57,603
Permissions change all the time.

33
00:02:57,603 --> 00:03:00,680
Here we remove Jordan from a Slack channel.

34
00:03:00,680 --> 00:03:10,295
His very next question no longer uses that channel, even if Slack's notification about it gets lost, because we check with the source every time.

35
00:03:10,295 --> 00:03:16,833
And when the runbook owner adds a new step, Priya's next question shows version 8, straight away.

36
00:03:16,833 --> 00:03:18,633
Nothing stale.

37
00:03:25,229 --> 00:03:27,153
Now for the compliance side.

38
00:03:27,153 --> 00:03:33,306
Maya asks in plain English: everything Jordan accessed in the payment gateway space, last thirty days.

39
00:03:33,306 --> 00:03:40,230
She sees each question, what was considered, what was allowed or denied and why, and which model answered.

40
00:03:40,230 --> 00:03:42,537
She can't read answers by default.

41
00:03:42,537 --> 00:03:48,306
To see one, she has to give a reason, and that reveal is logged too.

42
00:03:48,306 --> 00:03:50,999
The whole log is chained and sealed.

43
00:03:50,999 --> 00:03:54,845
"Verify audit chain" checks every record and every stored answer.

44
00:03:54,845 --> 00:04:00,614
And if someone edits a single line on disk, or deletes one, verification catches it.

45
00:04:08,569 --> 00:04:13,954
People don't want yet another app, so the Brain also lives inside Tencent WorkBuddy.

46
00:04:13,954 --> 00:04:23,954
Each connector carries its own signed token, so the AI in the middle can't decide who you are: Jordan's connector gets Jordan's answer, Ken's gets Ken's.

47
00:04:23,954 --> 00:04:27,800
And Maya can run her audit checks from WorkBuddy too.

48
00:04:31,519 --> 00:04:36,519
Underneath it all is Walnut, the evidence engine we built before this hackathon.

49
00:04:36,519 --> 00:04:47,289
Every value it touches carries a pointer back to where it came from, and you can follow that pointer, like this, from a fact to the exact document.

50
00:04:47,289 --> 00:04:51,135
The Internal Brain reuses that engine just as it is.

51
00:05:07,970 --> 00:05:16,431
Two checks before the model sees anything, a grounding check after it answers, and an audit record written before anything is shown.

52
00:05:16,431 --> 00:05:20,662
In this demo the answers are quoted straight from the sources.

53
00:05:20,662 --> 00:05:26,431
Add a TokenHub key, and Tencent Hy3 writes them instead, through exactly the same checks.

54
00:05:29,620 --> 00:05:39,235
We attacked it on purpose: nearly six thousand adversarial requests, zero leaks; four hundred paired answers, zero differences; and every scenario covered by automated tests.

55
00:05:40,269 --> 00:05:50,654
We used Tencent CodeBuddy to map the trust boundaries, build the WorkBuddy connector, write tests and review our security, and it found real bugs in our code.

56
00:05:50,654 --> 00:05:53,731
Six sessions, every transcript kept in the repository.

57
00:05:53,731 --> 00:05:55,531
Thanks for watching!
